#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133576 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "40bbc65e-5ee4-5ce6-aaf7-d0f9968c0c51" :knowledge-point/title "Representing a Double Integral as a Repeated Integral When the Limits are Given"}] :question/id #uuid "792b0c63-15eb-5b0e-b5d6-01ae5b68eeca" :question/math-academy-id "e-7134" :question/problem "If $D$ is the finite region enclosed by the curves $y=\\sqrt{x-2}$ and $y=1-x$ from $y=0$ to $y=1,$ as shown below, then what is $\\displaystyle \\iint\\limits_D (x^2y+xy^2) \\, \\mathrm{d}A$ expressed as a repeated integral?\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14055472/assets/0e4cf57408f9ca18dc4613317b8b9bc8b3149da0bf273ad03a8a8a48a6e6f454.png)" :question/worked-solution "First, let's represent $D$ as a type II region. Writing $y=\\sqrt{x-2}$ and $y=1-x$ in terms of $y,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y=\\sqrt{x-2} & \\,⟹\\,x={y}^{2}+2 \\\\ y=1-x & \\,⟹\\,x=1-y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo determine the left and right functions, we draw a horizontal arrow from left to right through the region, as usual.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14055472/assets/f0e531c612c84aa0260b318aa1fbe21c9089270b8c4a1079e08583b7fc5a5d90.png)\n\nAs a result, we get\n\n$$\nD = \\bigg\\{ (x,y) \\: : \\: 0 \\leq y \\leq 1, \\quad 1-y \\leq x  \\leq y^2+2 \\bigg\\}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can express the integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}({x}^{2}y+x{y}^{2})\\,dA & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{1-y}^{{y}^{2}+2}({x}^{2}y+x{y}^{2})\\,dx\\,dy\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133577 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "2ccbe70d-24f4-5e9c-8536-9ccab99f571b" :knowledge-point/title "Representing a Double Integral as a Repeated Integral When the Limits are Not Given"}] :question/id #uuid "e005ebb9-6c14-529e-be8f-9b837e45eef1" :question/math-academy-id "e-7135" :question/problem "Consider the finite region $D$ in the $xy$-plane enclosed between the curves $y=x^2$ and $y=3-2x$ below the line $y=3.$ What is $\\displaystyle \\iint\\limits_{D} f\\left( {x,y} \\right) \\, \\mathrm{d}A\\:$ expressed as a repeated integral?" :question/worked-solution "First, let's make a sketch of our region.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14055472/assets/ffbe0db1ae1bb082e63b0d97324b43dd78334dfbd1e88aaa0744be039dd0efc6.png)\n\nWe need to represent $D$ as a type II region. Writing $y=x^2$ and $y=3-2x$ in terms of $y,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y={x}^{2} & \\,⟹\\,x=\\sqrt{y} \\\\ y=3-2x & \\,⟹\\,x=\\frac{3-y}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's determine the limits of integration.\n\n- To determine the $y$-limits, we need to find the $y$-coordinates of the points at which the curves intersect. So, we set the following equation and solve it for $y\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{y} & =\\frac{3-y}{2} \\\\ 4y & =(3-y)^{2} \\\\ 4y & =9-6y+{y}^{2} \\\\ {y}^{2}-10y+9 & =0 \\\\ (y-1)(y-9) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, since our region lies below the line $y=3,$ the $y$-limits are \n\n$$\n1 \\le y \\le 3.\n$$\n\n- To determine the left and right functions, we draw a horizontal arrow from left to right through the region, as usual.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14055472/assets/3c56798a33ee2da6d928174b67546945be4bf3594d15e89f3ca39119dd4e2c62.png)\n\nAs a result, we get\n\n$$\nD = \\big\\{ (x,y) \\: : \\: 1 \\leq y \\leq 3, \\quad \\dfrac{3-y}{2} \\leq x  \\leq \\sqrt{y} \\big\\}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can express the integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\,dA & ={\\int }_{1}^{3}{\\int }_{(3-y)/2}^{\\sqrt{y}}f(x,y)\\,dx\\,dy\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133578 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "fdce40e5-8ffe-52cb-8a8e-ac8eaea982f0" :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating a Repeated Integral Defined Over a Type II Region"}] :question/id #uuid "ebd5bc16-2df2-5014-a1a2-bdebf2afa195" :question/math-academy-id "e-7136" :question/problem "Calculate $\\displaystyle \\int_{\\sqrt{\\pi}}^{\\sqrt{2\\pi}} \\int_0^{y^2} \\frac{\\sin y^2}{y} \\, \\mathrm{d}x \\,\\mathrm{d}y.$" :question/worked-solution "The integral is defined over a type II region.\n\nFirst, we evaluate the inner integral by integrating with respect to $x$, treating $y$ as a constant:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{\\sqrt{\\pi }}^{\\sqrt{2\\pi }}{\\int }_{0}^{{y}^{2}}\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡{y}^{2}}{y}\\,dx\\,dy & ={\\int }_{\\sqrt{\\pi }}^{\\sqrt{2\\pi }}\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡{y}^{2}}{y}[{\\int }_{0}^{{y}^{2}}dx]\\,dy \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\sqrt{\\pi }}^{\\sqrt{2\\pi }}\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡{y}^{2}}{y}[x]_{0}^{{y}^{2}}\\,dy \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\sqrt{\\pi }}^{\\sqrt{2\\pi }}\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡{y}^{2}}{y}({y}^{2}-0)\\,dy \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\sqrt{\\pi }}^{\\sqrt{2\\pi }}y\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡{y}^{2}\\,dy\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we integrate with respect to $y\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{\\sqrt{\\pi }}^{\\sqrt{2\\pi }}y\\operatorname{sin}⁡{y}^{2}\\,dy & ={[-\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡{y}^{2}]}_{\\sqrt{\\pi }}^{\\sqrt{2\\pi }} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡2\\pi -\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\pi ) \\\\  & =-1\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133579 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "90f7718f-e014-5232-acb1-6773ab79f61d" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating a Double Integral Defined Over a Type II Region"}] :question/id #uuid "ad2ef01a-d49d-5b17-9066-d5c3970caf31" :question/math-academy-id "e-7137" :question/problem "Evaluate the double integral $\\displaystyle \\iint \\limits_{D} xy^2\\,\\mathrm{d}A,\\:$\nwhere $D$ is the region enclosed between the curves $x^2+y^2=4$ and $x=0,$ and above the line $y=-1,$ as shown in the picture.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14055472/assets/f93aeee59ad97fde0efceff342e32a19062187261abec865616bfa963f1084a6.png)" :question/worked-solution "Notice that $D$ is a type II region. Since $x \\ge 0,$ we obtain the following equations in terms of $y\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=4 & \\,⟹\\,x=\\sqrt{4-{y}^{2}} \\\\ x=0 & \\,⟹\\,x=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's determine the limits of integration.\n\n- To determine the $y$-limits, we need to find the $y$-coordinates of the points at which the curves intersect. So, we set the following equation and solve it for $y\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{4-{y}^{2}} & =0 \\\\ 4-{y}^{2} & =0 \\\\ {y}^{2} & =4 \\\\ y & =\\pm 2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, since our region lies above the line $y=-1,$ the $y$-limits are \n$-1 \\le y \\le 2.$\n\n- To determine the left and right functions, we draw a horizontal arrow from left to right through the region, as usual.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14055472/assets/8d77e3caa18191710e056b72ea54e5820e6244d9e90719222deed409d3992c5b.png)\n\nAs a result, we get\n\n$$\nD = \\left\\{ (x,y) \\: : \\: -1 \\leq y \\leq 2, \\quad 0 \\leq x  \\leq \\sqrt{4-y^2} \\right\\}.\n$$\n\nSo, we can express the integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\iint \\limits_{D} xy^2\\,\\mathrm{d}A = \\int_{-1}^2 \\int_0^{\\sqrt{4-y^2}}\\, xy^2\\, \\mathrm dx\\,   \\mathrm{d}  y\n$$\n\nFirst, we evaluate the inner integral by integrating with respect to $x$, treating $y$ as a constant:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-1}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{4-{y}^{2}}}\\,x{y}^{2}\\,dx\\,dy & ={\\int }_{-1}^{2}{y}^{2}[{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{4-{y}^{2}}}x\\,dx]\\,dy \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{2}{y}^{2}{[\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}]}_{0}^{\\sqrt{4-{y}^{2}}}\\,dy \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{2}\\frac{{y}^{2}}{2}(4-{y}^{2}-0)\\,dy \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{2}(2{y}^{2}-\\frac{{y}^{4}}{2})\\,dy\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we integrate with respect to $y\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-1}^{2}(2{y}^{2}-\\frac{{y}^{4}}{2})\\,dy & ={[\\frac{2}{3}{y}^{3}-\\frac{{y}^{5}}{10}]}_{-1}^{2} \\\\  & =(\\frac{16}{3}-\\frac{32}{10})-(-\\frac{2}{3}+\\frac{1}{10}) \\\\  & =\\frac{27}{10}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186332695 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "40bbc65e-5ee4-5ce6-aaf7-d0f9968c0c51" :knowledge-point/title "Representing a Double Integral as a Repeated Integral When the Limits are Given"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4cb22083-7269-5829-8376-2d34d691e5c2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{2}[{\\int }_{4-{y}^{2}}^{{y}^{2}-4}f(x,y)\\,dx]dy" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186332697} {:answer/id #uuid "a3dda242-850d-5efd-a9c3-d41fca498194" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{2}[{\\int }_{{y}^{2}-4}^{4-{y}^{2}}f(x,y)\\,dx]dy" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186332698} {:answer/id #uuid "aefb590b-1954-51e6-82ed-f44cbd2d9133" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{{y}^{2}-4}^{4-{y}^{2}}[{\\int }_{0}^{2}f(x,y)\\,dx]dy" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186332699} {:answer/id #uuid "48b83e21-4bc3-5cff-856f-a3dd79430ec3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{{y}^{2}-4}^{2}[{\\int }_{0}^{4-{y}^{2}}f(x,y)\\,dx]dy" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186332700} {:answer/id #uuid "57ced348-cbd8-5e7e-9e49-f9ec2640e0b8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{4-{y}^{2}}[{\\int }_{{y}^{2}-4}^{2}f(x,y)\\,dx]dy" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186332701}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a3dda242-850d-5efd-a9c3-d41fca498194" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{2}[{\\int }_{{y}^{2}-4}^{4-{y}^{2}}f(x,y)\\,dx]dy" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186332698} :answer-field/id #uuid "7fad1b46-1023-5aa2-9466-d27d00ead44f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186332696}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "19602799-cf55-5cdc-a41d-4ab0cc8e92dc" :question/math-academy-id "q-132402" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14055472/assets/bfa591e51f42e8e52b67e61c23b02ea69b03bdecc1c4b815370ce6b2b0b5f213.png)\n\nIf $D$ is the finite region enclosed by the $x$-axis and the curves $x={y}^{2}-4$ and $x=4-{y}^{2},$ as shown above, then ${∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\,dA=$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's represent $D$ as a type II region. To determine the left and right functions, we draw a horizontal arrow from left to right through the region, as usual.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14055472/assets/85d3faac9a27353a8ea5151b3a32f1065aecb02fd07e0b20727c35de0a7fb216.png)\n\nAs a result, we get\n\n$$\nD={(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le y\\le 2,\\,{y}^{2}-4\\le x\\le 4-{y}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can express the integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\,dA & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{{y}^{2}-4}^{4-{y}^{2}}f(x,y)\\,dx\\,dy \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}[{\\int }_{{y}^{2}-4}^{4-{y}^{2}}f(x,y)\\,dx]dy\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186332702 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "fdce40e5-8ffe-52cb-8a8e-ac8eaea982f0" :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating a Repeated Integral Defined Over a Type II Region"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d78e9ee2-047b-5e89-b606-60fc204aadad" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1+\\frac{1}{e}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186332704} {:answer/id #uuid "6c759950-3a42-51cc-993b-ab64b895ecc5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "e+1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186332705} {:answer/id #uuid "3fe6c62d-6e38-58fd-b085-86453a09e5bd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1-\\frac{1}{e}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186332706} {:answer/id #uuid "9964b396-9653-52ce-9579-6cadd757d39b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "e-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186332707} {:answer/id #uuid "58103bfa-a285-51e1-a2be-f5e7c605dc15" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "e-\\frac{1}{e}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186332708}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "58103bfa-a285-51e1-a2be-f5e7c605dc15" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "e-\\frac{1}{e}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186332708} :answer-field/id #uuid "26965548-0d00-5094-aca1-d03a9c036f40" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186332703}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "30f6dbff-f649-5725-b4bd-0bb3cfdf4187" :question/math-academy-id "q-132421" :question/problem "Calculate ${\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{sin}⁡y}{e}^{\\operatorname{cos}⁡y}\\,dx\\,dy.$" :question/worked-solution "The integral is defined over a type II region.\n\nFirst, we evaluate the inner integral by integrating with respect to $x$, treating $y$ as a constant:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{sin}⁡y}{e}^{\\operatorname{cos}⁡y}\\,dx\\,dy & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{e}^{\\operatorname{cos}⁡y}[{\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{sin}⁡y}dx]dy \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{e}^{\\operatorname{cos}⁡y}[x]_{0}^{\\operatorname{sin}⁡y}\\,dy \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{e}^{\\operatorname{cos}⁡y}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡y-0)\\,dy \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡y\\,{e}^{\\operatorname{cos}⁡y}\\,dy\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we integrate with respect to $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡y\\,{e}^{\\operatorname{cos}⁡y}\\,dy & =[-{e}^{\\operatorname{cos}⁡y}]_{0}^{\\pi } \\\\  & =-{e}^{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\pi }-(-{e}^{\\operatorname{cos}⁡0}) \\\\  & =-{e}^{-1}+{e}^{1} \\\\  & =e-\\frac{1}{e}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186332709 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "2ccbe70d-24f4-5e9c-8536-9ccab99f571b" :knowledge-point/title "Representing a Double Integral as a Repeated Integral When the Limits are Not Given"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b7dd10a0-cc46-5b59-87d3-9174e3a74ef6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{1}^{5-y/2}[{\\int }_{y-1}^{4}x\\sqrt{y}\\,dx]dy" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186332711} {:answer/id #uuid "9c9d2bce-761c-57cb-bed7-5eabbdc8ee3c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{y-1}^{4}[{\\int }_{1}^{5-y/2}x\\sqrt{y}\\,dx]dy" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186332712} {:answer/id #uuid "ed7083eb-ec17-5ebc-8585-9a22c4d3bbf8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{1}^{4}[{\\int }_{y-1}^{5-y/2}x\\sqrt{y}\\,dx]dy" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186332713} {:answer/id #uuid "cae9b901-9f03-5cca-92e8-1f6e00cc8cca" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{y-1}^{5-y/2}[{\\int }_{1}^{4}x\\sqrt{y}\\,dx]dy" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186332714} {:answer/id #uuid "6ed45d05-79e5-542d-9b49-2d95fe7a5a8c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{1}^{4}[{\\int }_{5-y/2}^{y-1}x\\sqrt{y}\\,dx]dy" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186332715}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ed7083eb-ec17-5ebc-8585-9a22c4d3bbf8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{1}^{4}[{\\int }_{y-1}^{5-y/2}x\\sqrt{y}\\,dx]dy" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186332713} :answer-field/id #uuid "5cbdac7b-e62c-5341-bf37-c98789712411" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186332710}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "0ea00186-dbdd-5fef-968b-f1e2bc005692" :question/math-academy-id "q-132499" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14055472/assets/6d6597bff5d28155e9a40f953b0ab0652f175e4d4d0aaf6f28c50e6d5bc907d2.png)\n\nIf $D$ is the finite region enclosed by the lines $x=y-1,\\,$ $x=5-\\frac{y}{2},$ and $y=1,$ as shown above, then ${∬}_{D}x\\sqrt{y}\\,dA=$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's represent $D$ as a type II region. \n\n- To determine the $y$-limits, we need to find the $y$-coordinates of the points at which the curves intersect. So, we set the following equation and solve it for $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-1 & =-\\frac{y}{2}+5 \\\\ \\frac{3}{2}y & =6 \\\\ y & =4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, $y$-limits are \n$1\\le y\\le 4.$\n\n- To determine the left and right functions, we draw a horizontal arrow from left to right through the region, as usual.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14055472/assets/e2de1b62c6f3d0a15c0b95e3204f7889f6fc1ffbe108bdba7871c150091f6977.png)\n\nAs a result, we get\n\n$$\nD={(x,y)\\,:\\,1\\le y\\le 4,\\,y-1\\le x\\le 5-\\frac{y}{2}}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can express the integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}x\\sqrt{y}\\,dA & ={\\int }_{1}^{4}{\\int }_{y-1}^{5-y/2}x\\sqrt{y}\\,dx\\,dy \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{4}[{\\int }_{y-1}^{5-y/2}x\\sqrt{y}\\,dx]dy\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186332716 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "2ccbe70d-24f4-5e9c-8536-9ccab99f571b" :knowledge-point/title "Representing a Double Integral as a Repeated Integral When the Limits are Not Given"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d1bd7153-962b-58dc-b185-f99438b0d090" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{(y-2)^{2}/2-2}^{2-{y}^{2}}[{\\int }_{0}^{2}x{y}^{2}\\,dx]dy" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186332718} {:answer/id #uuid "121344bf-66ee-5751-916b-6dbd64076beb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{(y-2)^{2}/2-2}^{2}[{\\int }_{0}^{2-{y}^{2}}x{y}^{2}\\,dx]dy" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186332719} {:answer/id #uuid "08722839-b6c5-58e2-ab01-33df1d039a44" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{2-{y}^{2}}[{\\int }_{(y-2)^{2}/2-2}^{2}x{y}^{2}\\,dx]dy" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186332720} {:answer/id #uuid "4afa6f7f-2b5d-586c-917c-026e65b88b60" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{2}[{\\int }_{2-{y}^{2}}^{(y-2)^{2}/2-2}x{y}^{2}\\,dx]dy" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186332721} {:answer/id #uuid "caba9513-bd38-5ea3-81cd-ccc7ee023ac5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{2}[{\\int }_{(y-2)^{2}/2-2}^{2-{y}^{2}}x{y}^{2}\\,dx]dy" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186332722}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "caba9513-bd38-5ea3-81cd-ccc7ee023ac5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{2}[{\\int }_{(y-2)^{2}/2-2}^{2-{y}^{2}}x{y}^{2}\\,dx]dy" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186332722} :answer-field/id #uuid "67ca20b8-81a8-5715-b683-2ef9e9f557f9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186332717}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "11862ce2-c090-5636-9ec3-c43c9808c1ec" :question/math-academy-id "q-132510" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14055472/assets/f390e83f737b3b20931df5e97a9ea0750b8b190acba14d1cd963aaab5175ba50.png)\n\nIf $D$ is the finite region enclosed between the curves $x=\\frac{1}{2}(y-2)^{2}-2$ and $x=2-{y}^{2},$ above the $x$-axis, as shown above, then ${∬}_{D}x{y}^{2}\\,dA=$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's represent $D$ as a type II region. \n\n- To determine the $y$-limits, we need to find the $y$-coordinates of the points at which the curves intersect. So, we set the following equation and solve it for $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}(y-2)^{2}-2 & =2-{y}^{2} \\\\ \\frac{1}{2}({y}^{2}-4y+4)+{y}^{2}-4 & =0 \\\\ {y}^{2}-4y+4+2{y}^{2}-8 & =0 \\\\ 3{y}^{2}-4y-4 & =0 \\\\ (3y+2)(y-2) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, since our region lies above the line $y=0,$ the $y$-limits are \n$0\\le y\\le 2.$,\n\n- To determine the left and right functions, we draw a horizontal arrow from left to right through the region, as usual.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14055472/assets/1b67c65c78dc847c2bf4bf53ad67f770a1406570548497edd55939c3a68a4c5c.png)\n\nAs a result, we get\n\n$$\nD={(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le y\\le 2,\\,\\frac{1}{2}(y-2)^{2}-2\\le x\\le 2-{y}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can express the integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}x{y}^{2}\\,dA & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{(y-2)^{2}/2-2}^{2-{y}^{2}}x{y}^{2}\\,dx\\,dy \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}[{\\int }_{(y-2)^{2}/2-2}^{2-{y}^{2}}x{y}^{2}\\,dx]dy\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186332723 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "2ccbe70d-24f4-5e9c-8536-9ccab99f571b" :knowledge-point/title "Representing a Double Integral as a Repeated Integral When the Limits are Not Given"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c13615d8-7159-5619-b507-2d9c2a8aef15" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{{y}^{2}}^{1}[{\\int }_{0}^{2-{y}^{2}}\\sqrt{\\frac{x}{y+1}}\\,dx]dy" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186332725} {:answer/id #uuid "e3eddd40-50ac-569c-b853-35fe004a52a3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{{y}^{2}}^{2-{y}^{2}}[{\\int }_{0}^{1}\\sqrt{\\frac{x}{y+1}}\\,dx]dy" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186332726} {:answer/id #uuid "844ef872-5462-5357-b541-1227fd962258" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{1}[{\\int }_{{y}^{2}}^{2-{y}^{2}}\\sqrt{\\frac{x}{y+1}}\\,dx]dy" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186332727} {:answer/id #uuid "3f583e21-8385-5a29-afc9-45bff8108a33" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{1}[{\\int }_{2-{y}^{2}}^{{y}^{2}}\\sqrt{\\frac{x}{y+1}}\\,dx]dy" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186332728} {:answer/id #uuid "7d72a8cb-7ced-59f3-bb6c-4e384d778158" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{2-{y}^{2}}[{\\int }_{{y}^{2}}^{1}\\sqrt{\\frac{x}{y+1}}\\,dx]dy" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186332729}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "844ef872-5462-5357-b541-1227fd962258" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{1}[{\\int }_{{y}^{2}}^{2-{y}^{2}}\\sqrt{\\frac{x}{y+1}}\\,dx]dy" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186332727} :answer-field/id #uuid "796f1c92-4358-5fd5-9af4-4df637129f8f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186332724}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "3e86e174-5e1d-50e1-90b0-25915ef48280" :question/math-academy-id "q-132512" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14055472/assets/d90e750755a9520328e99583bfc80d9498ed34eb1695ba4ecd55b688d5b0216f.png)\n\nIf $D$ is the finite region enclosed between the curves $x={y}^{2}$ and $x=2-{y}^{2},$ above the $x$-axis, as shown above, then ${∬}_{D}\\sqrt{\\frac{x}{y+1}}\\,dA=$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's represent $D$ as a type II region. \n\n- To determine the $y$-limits, we need to find the $y$-coordinates of the points at which the curves intersect. So, we set the following equation and solve it for $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{2} & =2-{y}^{2} \\\\ 2{y}^{2} & =2 \\\\ {y}^{2} & =1 \\\\ y & =\\pm 1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, since our region lies above the line $y=0,$ the $y$-limits are \n$0\\le y\\le 1.$\n\n- To determine the left and right functions, we draw a horizontal arrow from left to right through the region, as usual.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14055472/assets/eeadcddee3bc966372613ded9304c723768e9b81cd0299ad14084a94c9348463.png)\n\nAs a result, we get\n\n$$\nD={(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le y\\le 1,\\,{y}^{2}\\le x\\le 2-{y}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can express the integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}\\sqrt{\\frac{x}{y+1}}\\,dA & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{{y}^{2}}^{2-{y}^{2}}\\sqrt{\\frac{x}{y+1}}\\,dx\\,dy \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}[{\\int }_{{y}^{2}}^{2-{y}^{2}}\\sqrt{\\frac{x}{y+1}}\\,dx]dy\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186332730 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "90f7718f-e014-5232-acb1-6773ab79f61d" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating a Double Integral Defined Over a Type II Region"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "327c164b-7f45-55af-b466-bc9ff6cf052a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186332732} {:answer/id #uuid "9ffd597e-f8d4-5ec2-93f1-0a20dab32d11" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186332733} {:answer/id #uuid "444fac14-f20e-5218-b051-c8c326242701" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186332734} {:answer/id #uuid "d0d14262-dd20-5cfa-a9f8-6b9a9a7ad393" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186332735} {:answer/id #uuid "f6281426-a22c-56a4-b7cd-d229021e250e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186332736}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "444fac14-f20e-5218-b051-c8c326242701" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186332734} :answer-field/id #uuid "0e9b8828-4a18-5a98-a51e-db91abf63004" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186332731}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "1c5ca9ca-aaf0-57a5-9113-ae2d9b07398d" :question/math-academy-id "q-132527" :question/problem "Calculate ${∬}_{D}y\\,dA,$ where $D={(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le y\\le 1,\\,2{y}^{2}\\le x\\le {y}^{2}+1}.$" :question/worked-solution "The integral is defined over a type II region $D={(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le y\\le 1,\\,2{y}^{2}\\le x\\le {y}^{2}+1}.$\n\nSo, we can express the integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}y\\,dA & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{2{y}^{2}}^{{y}^{2}+1}y\\,dx\\,dy\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFirst, we evaluate the inner integral by integrating with respect to $x$, treating $y$ as a constant:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{2{y}^{2}}^{{y}^{2}+1}y\\,dx\\,dy & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}y[{\\int }_{2{y}^{2}}^{{y}^{2}+1}\\,dx]dy \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}y[\\,x\\,]_{2{y}^{2}}^{{y}^{2}+1}\\,dy \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}y({y}^{2}+1-2{y}^{2})dy \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(y-{y}^{3})\\,dy\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we integrate with respect to $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{1}(y-{y}^{3})\\,dy & ={[\\frac{1}{2}{y}^{2}-\\frac{1}{4}{y}^{4}]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}-\\frac{1}{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186332737 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "90f7718f-e014-5232-acb1-6773ab79f61d" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating a Double Integral Defined Over a Type II Region"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "17b234b1-8ffc-5360-9b5e-785ffd382aee" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{7}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186332739} {:answer/id #uuid "563a7936-6784-57ac-87a6-ba67f7c55bde" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186332740} {:answer/id #uuid "51ba1e79-03e1-55ed-9700-630c49339e1d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186332741} {:answer/id #uuid "ae1521a1-2e01-5da1-972d-4a0cf33595a1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186332742} {:answer/id #uuid "cdcaecb2-46b2-5639-ba48-d4c2a6a4feb8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186332743}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "17b234b1-8ffc-5360-9b5e-785ffd382aee" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{7}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186332739} :answer-field/id #uuid "ae60d52d-9b12-5be9-bf60-0895f7fb9630" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186332738}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "b686fe8e-540c-5e6e-855f-cc2825f800a7" :question/math-academy-id "q-132531" :question/problem "Calculate ${∬}_{D}\\frac{{x}^{2}}{y}\\,dA,$ where $D={(x,y)\\,:\\,1\\le y\\le 2,\\,0\\le x\\le {y}^{2}}.$" :question/worked-solution "The integral is defined over a type II region $D={(x,y)\\,:\\,1\\le y\\le 2,\\,0\\le x\\le {y}^{2}}.$\n\nSo, we can express the integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}\\frac{{x}^{2}}{y}\\,dA & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{{y}^{2}}\\frac{{x}^{2}}{y}\\,dx\\,dy\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFirst, we evaluate the inner integral by integrating with respect to $x$, treating $y$ as a constant:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{{y}^{2}}\\frac{{x}^{2}}{y}\\,dx\\,dy & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}\\frac{1}{y}[{\\int }_{0}^{{y}^{2}}{x}^{2}\\,dx]dy \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}\\frac{1}{y}{[\\frac{1}{3}{x}^{3}]}_{0}^{{y}^{2}}\\,dy \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}\\frac{1}{y}(\\frac{1}{3}{y}^{6}-0)dy \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}\\frac{1}{3}{y}^{5}\\,dy\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we integrate with respect to $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{2}\\frac{1}{3}{y}^{5}\\,dy & =\\frac{1}{3}{\\int }_{1}^{2}{y}^{5}\\,dy \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}{[\\frac{1}{6}{y}^{6}]}_{1}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}(\\frac{32}{3}-\\frac{1}{6}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot \\frac{21}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{7}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186332744 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "90f7718f-e014-5232-acb1-6773ab79f61d" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating a Double Integral Defined Over a Type II Region"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2758e88f-d1df-5ec6-840f-df02d615ad30" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{16}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186332746} {:answer/id #uuid "cef645cc-499c-57d9-9907-f7d9e4df8858" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "16" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186332747} {:answer/id #uuid "cac34dfe-54b3-5c46-9881-eaa692a22f2f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186332748} {:answer/id #uuid "392ea7d1-3a92-5a9f-b538-070778a2527b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{8}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186332749} {:answer/id #uuid "3d8dc75d-93a5-5526-97b2-4b22a8aefdf0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186332750}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "cef645cc-499c-57d9-9907-f7d9e4df8858" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "16" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186332747} :answer-field/id #uuid "c32b5e54-42da-531c-b5b3-821a6d967b9e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186332745}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "4f1f896d-423d-5f07-bb8f-0a0deac07744" :question/math-academy-id "q-132562" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14055472/assets/d5f54020da277980a58151bf2a85911365e67ecae6832881e03ac8b9b6431e6a.png)\n\nEvaluate the double integral ${∬}_{D}4x\\,dA,\\,$\nwhere $D$ is the region enclosed between the curves $16{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=16$ and $4{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=16,$ above the $x$-axis, as shown in the picture." :question/worked-solution "Notice that $D$ is a type II region. Since $x\\ge 0,$ we obtain the following equations in terms of $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}16{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=16 & \\,⟹\\,x=\\sqrt{1-\\frac{{y}^{2}}{16}} \\\\ 4{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=16 & \\,⟹\\,x=\\sqrt{4-\\frac{{y}^{2}}{4}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's determine the limits of integration.\n\n- To determine the $y$-limits, we need to find the $y$-coordinates of the points at which the curves intersect. So, we set the following equation and solve it for $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{1-\\frac{{y}^{2}}{16}} & =\\sqrt{4-\\frac{{y}^{2}}{4}} \\\\ 1-\\frac{{y}^{2}}{16} & =4-\\frac{{y}^{2}}{4} \\\\ \\frac{3}{16}{y}^{2} & =3 \\\\ {y}^{2} & =16 \\\\ y & =\\pm 4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince our region lies above the $x$-axis, the $y$-limits are \n$0\\le y\\le 4.$\n\n- To determine the left and right functions, we draw a horizontal arrow from left to right through the region, as usual.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14055472/assets/d8b8b05e9004f6b25f635e9c39421a8e8e27831a26a22273cf88572df89ffc45.png)\n\nAs a result, we get\n\n$$\nD={(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le y\\le 4,\\,\\sqrt{1-\\frac{{y}^{2}}{16}}\\le x\\le \\sqrt{4-\\frac{{y}^{2}}{4}}}.\n$$\n\nSo, we can express the integral as follows:\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}4x\\,dA={\\int }_{0}^{4}{\\int }_{\\sqrt{1-{y}^{2}/16}}^{\\sqrt{4-{y}^{2}/4}}\\,4x\\,dx\\,dy\n$$\n\nFirst, we evaluate the inner integral by integrating with respect to $x$, treating $y$ as a constant:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{4}{\\int }_{\\sqrt{1-{y}^{2}/16}}^{\\sqrt{4-{y}^{2}/4}}\\,4x\\,dx\\,dy & ={\\int }_{0}^{4}2[{\\int }_{\\sqrt{1-{y}^{2}/16}}^{\\sqrt{4-{y}^{2}/4}}\\,2x\\,dx]dy \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{4}\\,2{[\\,{x}^{2}\\,]}_{\\sqrt{1-{y}^{2}/16}}^{\\sqrt{4-{y}^{2}/4}}\\text{ }dy \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{4}\\,2[4-\\frac{{y}^{2}}{4}-(1-\\frac{{y}^{2}}{16})]dy \\\\  & =2{\\int }_{0}^{4}\\,[3-\\frac{3}{16}{y}^{2}]dy \\\\  & =6{\\int }_{0}^{4}\\,(1-\\frac{1}{16}{y}^{2})dy\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we integrate with respect to $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}6{\\int }_{0}^{4}\\,(1-\\frac{{y}^{2}}{16})dy & =6{[y-\\frac{{y}^{3}}{48}]}_{0}^{4} \\\\  & =6(4-\\frac{64}{48}) \\\\  & =6(4-\\frac{4}{3}) \\\\  & =6\\cdot \\frac{8}{3} \\\\  & =16\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186332751 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "fdce40e5-8ffe-52cb-8a8e-ac8eaea982f0" :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating a Repeated Integral Defined Over a Type II Region"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0db3bbd6-fb95-526a-980f-021c74948e6d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\pi -\\frac{1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186332753} {:answer/id #uuid "3faf5f38-2b8a-52c9-bef0-38aa6c7f36a2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{12}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186332754} {:answer/id #uuid "ed7f1485-131c-5911-a020-8c7d15975be7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186332755} {:answer/id #uuid "974bbd31-56fb-5e5e-8987-917051a23239" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186332756} {:answer/id #uuid "6a4889b1-c565-57d9-a289-b4047eeffc57" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{2}+\\frac{1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186332757}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ed7f1485-131c-5911-a020-8c7d15975be7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186332755} :answer-field/id #uuid "7911ff55-b633-5c4a-a16a-c106f9006227" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186332752}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "9c5423a0-a399-5841-a575-289a1476314b" :question/math-academy-id "q-37996" :question/problem "${\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{{y}^{2}}^{y}(1-xy)\\,dx\\,dy=$" :question/worked-solution "The integral is defined over a type II region.\n\nFirst, we evaluate the inner integral by integrating with respect to $x$, treating $y$ as a constant:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{{y}^{2}}^{y}(1-xy)\\,dx\\,dy & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}[{\\int }_{{y}^{2}}^{y}(1-xy)\\,dx]\\,dy \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{[x-\\frac{y{x}^{2}}{2}]}_{{y}^{2}}^{y}\\,dy \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(y-\\frac{{y}^{3}}{2}-({y}^{2}-\\frac{{y}^{5}}{2}))\\,dy \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(y-\\frac{{y}^{3}}{2}-{y}^{2}+\\frac{{y}^{5}}{2})\\,dy\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we integrate with respect to $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{1}(y-\\frac{{y}^{3}}{2}-{y}^{2}+\\frac{{y}^{5}}{2})\\,dy & ={[\\frac{{y}^{2}}{2}-\\frac{{y}^{4}}{8}-\\frac{{y}^{3}}{3}+\\frac{{y}^{6}}{12}]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}-\\frac{1}{8}-\\frac{1}{3}+\\frac{1}{12}-0 \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{8}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134222 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "40bbc65e-5ee4-5ce6-aaf7-d0f9968c0c51" :knowledge-point/title "Representing a Double Integral as a Repeated Integral When the Limits are Given"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "894b2a6e-4ff7-5edc-8a67-ca3f02cfe95a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "∫_{0}^{y/2}∫_{-y}^{4}(x^{2} + y^{2})dxdy" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186131775} {:answer/id #uuid "a604efcf-9afb-5962-afe3-a42ce165d446" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "∫_{-y}^{y/2}∫_{0}^{4}(x^{2} + y^{2})dxdy" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186131776} {:answer/id #uuid "317ca957-9dfd-546a-85c3-d566372240ec" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "∫_{0}^{4}∫_{-y}^{y/2}(x^{2} + y^{2})dxdy" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186131777} {:answer/id #uuid "493fee45-3b3a-5755-a867-c20d0c5504a0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "∫_{-y}^{4}∫_{0}^{y/2}(x^{2} + y^{2})dxdy" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186131778} {:answer/id #uuid "68619434-3053-5ee8-8c59-7d86ca427e59" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "∫_{0}^{4}∫_{y/2}^{-y}(x^{2} + y^{2})dxdy" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186131779}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "317ca957-9dfd-546a-85c3-d566372240ec" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "∫_{0}^{4}∫_{-y}^{y/2}(x^{2} + y^{2})dxdy" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186131777} :answer-field/id #uuid "fbfa7fea-fd46-5e44-a86e-413fd7767507" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186131774}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "978f3c20-c798-5873-9aff-561bd561582a" :question/math-academy-id "q-40575" :question/problem "Consider the finite region $D$ in the $xy$-plane enclosed between the lines $y=2x$ and $y=-x$ from $y=0$ to $y=4.$ What is ${∬}_{D}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})\\,dA$ expressed as a repeated integral?" :question/worked-solution "First, let's represent $D$ as a type II region. Writing $y=2x$ and $y=-x$ in terms of $y,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y=2x & \\,⟹\\,x=\\frac{x}{2} \\\\ y=-x & \\,⟹\\,x=-y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo determine the left and right functions, we draw a horizontal arrow from left to right through the region, as usual.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14055472/assets/df512140098a6fc6adc03e4d98199896cf15ed1cdd40578aea14ff1e83dfeba3.png)\n\nAs a result, we get\n\n$$\nD={(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le y\\le 4,\\,-y\\le x\\le \\frac{y}{2}}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can express the integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})\\,dA & ={\\int }_{0}^{4}{\\int }_{-y}^{y/2}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})\\,dx\\,dy\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134223 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "2ccbe70d-24f4-5e9c-8536-9ccab99f571b" :knowledge-point/title "Representing a Double Integral as a Repeated Integral When the Limits are Not Given"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a49b112d-d43c-5793-aae5-a0e8d23a1dc5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "∫_{y/2}^{2 - y}[∫_{0}^{4/3}xydx]dy" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186131781} {:answer/id #uuid "e0df9bbf-af20-540f-9b7d-4ec29ce46e46" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "∫_{2 - y}^{y/2}[∫_{0}^{4/3}xydx]dy" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186131782} {:answer/id #uuid "14b51ba3-881d-56a4-9275-73e7fd267662" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "∫_{0}^{4/3}[∫_{y/2}^{2 - y}xydx]dy" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186131783} {:answer/id #uuid "2b9957a1-8302-55a8-89bd-99732feb24cb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "∫_{0}^{y/2}[∫_{0}^{2 - y}xydx]dy" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186131784} {:answer/id #uuid "76c605d6-bef0-58a6-b81e-a85be78915fa" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "∫_{0}^{4/3}[∫_{2 - y}^{y/2}xydx]dy" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186131785}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "14b51ba3-881d-56a4-9275-73e7fd267662" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "∫_{0}^{4/3}[∫_{y/2}^{2 - y}xydx]dy" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186131783} :answer-field/id #uuid "2f173185-0969-5f7b-88eb-cc3b2904fc1b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186131780}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "89052f85-16e5-5038-8862-a854c23cc74d" :question/math-academy-id "q-40596" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14055472/assets/c752eb32fc94ae630ef3998c81a6b9b0af5c222a367f87db8bbacb877716c16c.png)\n\nIf $D$ is the finite region enclosed by the lines $x=2-y,\\,$ $x=\\frac{y}{2},$ and $y=0,$ as shown above, then ${∬}_{D}xy\\,dA=$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's represent $D$ as a type II region. \n\n- To determine the $y$-limits, we need to find the $y$-coordinates of the points at which the curves intersect. So, we set the following equation and solve it for $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2-y & =\\frac{y}{2} \\\\ -\\frac{3}{2}y & =-2 \\\\ y & =\\frac{4}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, $y$-limits are \n$0\\le y\\le \\frac{4}{3}.$\n\n- To determine the left and right functions, we draw a horizontal arrow from left to right through the region, as usual.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14055472/assets/e6c9332f8f59e2561af9ba5aa89311263fb772dc987ed0b15d91a6d5eea5cf8e.png)\n\nAs a result, we get\n\n$$\nD={(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le y\\le \\frac{4}{3},\\,\\frac{y}{2}\\le x\\le 2-y}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can express the integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}xy\\,dA & ={\\int }_{0}^{4/3}{\\int }_{y/2}^{2-y}xy\\,dx\\,dy \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{4/3}[{\\int }_{y/2}^{2-y}xy\\,dx]dy\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186332758 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "90f7718f-e014-5232-acb1-6773ab79f61d" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating a Double Integral Defined Over a Type II Region"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1831ae25-1d51-5418-8e56-316433b45b16" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{7}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186332760} {:answer/id #uuid "ce67e1ef-dcbb-5755-8ed4-51bdcb66eda3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{7}{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186332761} {:answer/id #uuid "d6a5ee6b-62ea-5ce5-99fe-49fd2bc7e5ca" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186332762} {:answer/id #uuid "d650f2a8-2f5e-5d66-980b-531f0c04b5b7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186332763} {:answer/id #uuid "d9c26741-e06a-5a76-89c3-e1aa628250dd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186332764}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ce67e1ef-dcbb-5755-8ed4-51bdcb66eda3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{7}{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186332761} :answer-field/id #uuid "02ba99cd-cd32-5955-8db9-be3261dc6513" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186332759}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "7a5b3321-cf12-51db-a631-dd5473f67f10" :question/math-academy-id "q-40680" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14055472/assets/a135b0766239bcd6e9cacc324572486e66e1c859ecd28a4d59ef7795cc511ed4.png)\n\nEvaluate the double integral ${∬}_{D}xy\\,dA,\\,$\nwhere $D$ is the region enclosed between the curves ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=4$ and $x=0,$ above the $x$-axis and below the line $y=1,$ as shown in the picture." :question/worked-solution "Notice that $D$ is a type II region. Since $x\\ge 0,$ we obtain the following equations in terms of $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=4 & \\,⟹\\,x=\\sqrt{4-{y}^{2}} \\\\ x=0 & \\,⟹\\,x=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's determine the limits of integration.\n\n- To determine the $y$-limits, we need to find the $y$-coordinates of the points at which the curves intersect. So, we set the following equation and solve it for $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{4-{y}^{2}} & =0 \\\\ 4-{y}^{2} & =0 \\\\ {y}^{2} & =4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, since our region lies above the $x$-axis and below the line $y=1,$ the $y$-limits are \n$0\\le y\\le 1.$\n\n- To determine the left and right functions, we draw a horizontal arrow from left to right through the region, as usual.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14055472/assets/373286c03cc595535a7f04987e7454be2fac547d769341343f05ea67ee0b001d.png)\n\nAs a result, we get\n\n$$\nD={(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le y\\le 1,\\,0\\le x\\le \\sqrt{4-{y}^{2}}}.\n$$\n\nSo, we can express the integral as follows:\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}xy\\,dA={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{4-{y}^{2}}}\\,xy\\,dx\\,dy\n$$\n\nFirst, we evaluate the inner integral by integrating with respect to $x$, treating $y$ as a constant:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{4-{y}^{2}}}\\,xy\\,dx\\,dy & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}[{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{4-{y}^{2}}}\\,xy\\,dx]\\,dy \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}\\,{[\\,\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{2}y\\,]}_{0}^{\\sqrt{4-{y}^{2}}}\\text{ }dy \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}\\,\\frac{1}{2}(4-{y}^{2})ydy \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}\\,\\frac{1}{2}(4y-{y}^{3})dy\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we integrate with respect to $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{1}\\,\\frac{1}{2}(4y-{y}^{3})dy & ={[\\frac{1}{2}(2{y}^{2}-\\frac{{y}^{4}}{4})]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(2-\\frac{1}{4})-\\frac{1}{2}(0) \\\\  & =\\frac{7}{8}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186332765 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "40bbc65e-5ee4-5ce6-aaf7-d0f9968c0c51" :knowledge-point/title "Representing a Double Integral as a Repeated Integral When the Limits are Given"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "69c6dae4-39c1-5972-84e2-3caf3dd62aea" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{2}[{\\int }_{{y}^{2}/2}^{6-{y}^{2}}f(x,y)\\,dx]dy" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186332767} {:answer/id #uuid "c603822b-6bb1-5b04-badf-84155da40d3a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{6-{y}^{2}}[{\\int }_{2}^{{y}^{2}/2}f(x,y)\\,dx]dy" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186332768} {:answer/id #uuid "5c7f83d0-fcfe-5eb7-9614-d4a223458fd6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{{y}^{2}/2}^{6-{y}^{2}}[{\\int }_{0}^{2}f(x,y)\\,dx]dy" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186332769} {:answer/id #uuid "353f51ee-226c-511e-8831-b58fcdea5490" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{2}[{\\int }_{6-{y}^{2}}^{{y}^{2}/2}f(x,y)\\,dx]dy" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186332770} {:answer/id #uuid "89d9529c-0e18-5594-96c6-6f9a52ade782" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{{y}^{2}/2}[{\\int }_{2}^{6-{y}^{2}}f(x,y)\\,dx]dy" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186332771}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "69c6dae4-39c1-5972-84e2-3caf3dd62aea" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{2}[{\\int }_{{y}^{2}/2}^{6-{y}^{2}}f(x,y)\\,dx]dy" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186332767} :answer-field/id #uuid "95490564-6e43-5c4b-9ca3-8ccbeacb6212" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186332766}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "01fbeb37-0afa-51c2-b623-3242c662194e" :question/math-academy-id "q-81232" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14055472/assets/5197029ed6803a39bf6d6d14bbdd8b3a71d8905e854bf3842305828280cbc39b.png)\n\nIf $D$ is the finite region enclosed by the curves $x=\\frac{{y}^{2}}{2},\\,$ $x=6-{y}^{2},$ and $y=0,$ as shown above, then ${∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\,dA=$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's represent $D$ as a type II region. To determine the left and right functions, we draw a horizontal arrow from left to right through the region, as usual.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14055472/assets/1761963e320e603b9c94ea4bab2df6f6ee8cc1b92cc0ca45bc84c8aeda1d3a11.png)\n\nAs a result, we get\n\n$$\nD={(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le y\\le 2,\\,\\frac{{y}^{2}}{2}\\le x\\le 6-{y}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can express the integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\,dA & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{{y}^{2}/2}^{6-{y}^{2}}f(x,y)\\,dx\\,dy \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}[{\\int }_{{y}^{2}/2}^{6-{y}^{2}}f(x,y)\\,dx]dy\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186332772 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "40bbc65e-5ee4-5ce6-aaf7-d0f9968c0c51" :knowledge-point/title "Representing a Double Integral as a Repeated Integral When the Limits are Given"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ddec68e4-d6c4-5393-bf36-c522c9d3d1da" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{3-(y-3)^{2}}^{(y-3)^{2}+4}[{\\int }_{0}^{3}f(x,y)\\,dx]dy" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186332774} {:answer/id #uuid "38541ad2-d5ec-5364-9748-b3de88135016" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{3}[{\\int }_{(y-3)^{2}+4}^{3-(y-3)^{2}}f(x,y)\\,dx]dy" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186332775} {:answer/id #uuid "fa367df2-84c3-58af-8655-6c84c5620c4b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{(y-3)^{2}+4}[{\\int }_{3}^{3-(y-3)^{2}}f(x,y)\\,dx]dy" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186332776} {:answer/id #uuid "3995f3d4-28f7-57ca-ba3d-3efb139b50af" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{3-(y-3)^{2}}[{\\int }_{3}^{(y-4)^{2}+4}f(x,y)\\,dx]dy" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186332777} {:answer/id #uuid "c2e9f529-3cbd-58ca-a67f-8e4ef0c98df9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{3}[{\\int }_{3-(y-3)^{2}}^{(y-3)^{2}+4}f(x,y)\\,dx]dy" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186332778}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c2e9f529-3cbd-58ca-a67f-8e4ef0c98df9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{3}[{\\int }_{3-(y-3)^{2}}^{(y-3)^{2}+4}f(x,y)\\,dx]dy" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186332778} :answer-field/id #uuid "8d4a7495-4d32-549a-9853-9ea81c81c0f9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186332773}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "97d41871-0fb6-5b4b-9d4a-a5f8b16e5972" :question/math-academy-id "q-81234" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14055472/assets/5df807dc424366c043211f7d3a1667bc2c9888f24292856ed99a07d7ac586a94.png)\n\nIf $D$ is the finite region enclosed by the curves $x=3-(y-3)^{2},\\,$ $x=(y-3)^{2}+4,$ $y=0,$ and $y=3,$ as shown above, then ${∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\,dA=$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's represent $D$ as a type II region. To determine the left and right functions, we draw a horizontal arrow from left to right through the region, as usual.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14055472/assets/3191f16e4c72739688fa4243b44a374a027b307b6e4d419217545e924e1c7a4a.png)\n\nAs a result, we get\n\n$$\nD={(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le y\\le 3,\\,3-(y-3)^{2}\\le x\\le (y-3)^{2}+4}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can express the integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\,dA & ={\\int }_{0}^{3}{\\int }_{3-(y-3)^{2}}^{(y-3)^{2}+4}f(x,y)\\,dx\\,dy \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{3}[{\\int }_{3-(y-3)^{2}}^{(y-3)^{2}+4}f(x,y)\\,dx]dy\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186332779 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "40bbc65e-5ee4-5ce6-aaf7-d0f9968c0c51" :knowledge-point/title "Representing a Double Integral as a Repeated Integral When the Limits are Given"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3d44c874-2999-549d-88fe-5515e5b98c7c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{{y}^{2}+2}{\\int }_{1}^{\\sqrt{y}}\\frac{xy}{1+{y}^{2}}\\,dx\\,dy" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186332781} {:answer/id #uuid "f35d13dc-cb15-5954-a279-5e491440e715" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{y}}{\\int }_{1}^{{y}^{2}+2}\\frac{xy}{1+{y}^{2}}\\,dx\\,dy" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186332782} {:answer/id #uuid "d48ba639-3036-5f09-845b-fdc141700694" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{\\sqrt{y}}^{{y}^{2}+2}\\frac{xy}{1+{y}^{2}}\\,dx\\,dy" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186332783} {:answer/id #uuid "7cab6828-6212-5c15-b5b1-7c2b7d593b79" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{\\sqrt{y}}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{{y}^{2}+2}\\frac{xy}{1+{y}^{2}}\\,dx\\,dy" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186332784} {:answer/id #uuid "e1cf699a-266a-5603-a487-7ae6a8000841" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{{y}^{2}+2}^{\\sqrt{y}}\\frac{xy}{1+{y}^{2}}\\,dx\\,dy" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186332785}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d48ba639-3036-5f09-845b-fdc141700694" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{\\sqrt{y}}^{{y}^{2}+2}\\frac{xy}{1+{y}^{2}}\\,dx\\,dy" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186332783} :answer-field/id #uuid "90519ca9-201c-56ba-9ed7-2533133c0a0b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186332780}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "9f0dcf3b-fd2c-5538-bc97-3c2c49455a71" :question/math-academy-id "q-81260" :question/problem "Consider the finite region $D$ in the $xy$-plane enclosed between the curves $y={x}^{2}$ and $y=\\sqrt{x-2}$ from $y=0$ to $y=1.$ What is ${∬}_{D}\\frac{xy}{1+{y}^{2}}\\,dA$ expressed as a repeated integral?" :question/worked-solution "First, let's represent $D$ as a type II region. Writing $y={x}^{2}$ and $y=\\sqrt{x-2}$ in terms of $y,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y={x}^{2} & \\,⟹\\,x=\\sqrt{y} \\\\ y=\\sqrt{x-2} & \\,⟹\\,x={y}^{2}+2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo determine the left and right functions, we draw a horizontal arrow from left to right through the region, as usual.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14055472/assets/bb3249a9580e754c4ef9c33239c2a51a9b57ca9032a6acbeef7bbc532b2d4345.png)\n\nAs a result, we get\n\n$$\nD={(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le y\\le 1,\\,\\sqrt{y}\\le x\\le {y}^{2}+2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can express the integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}\\frac{xy}{1+{y}^{2}}\\,dA & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{\\sqrt{y}}^{{y}^{2}+2}\\frac{xy}{1+{y}^{2}}\\,dx\\,dy\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186332786 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "2ccbe70d-24f4-5e9c-8536-9ccab99f571b" :knowledge-point/title "Representing a Double Integral as a Repeated Integral When the Limits are Not Given"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "08453bf0-00d1-506c-b3b5-ae4e3c3e0923" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{1/y}^{2y}{\\int }_{\\sqrt{2}/2}^{2}f(x,y)\\,dx\\,dy" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186332788} {:answer/id #uuid "3ed2d29d-9fee-5db5-b818-fa8b741ea209" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{\\sqrt{2}/2}^{2}{\\int }_{2y}^{1/y}f(x,y)\\,dx\\,dy" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186332789} {:answer/id #uuid "18f70075-6f2f-5b4d-9ce3-f30d3f6e2cae" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{1/y}^{2}{\\int }_{\\sqrt{2}/2}^{2y}f(x,y)\\,dx\\,dy" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186332790} {:answer/id #uuid "d6a40839-69a4-5730-949f-5219f38ce864" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{\\sqrt{2}/2}^{1/y}{\\int }_{2}^{2y}f(x,y)\\,dx\\,dy" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186332791} {:answer/id #uuid "488ccd76-6a0f-5f9e-a4e7-e1c42b254497" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{\\sqrt{2}/2}^{2}{\\int }_{1/y}^{2y}f(x,y)\\,dx\\,dy" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186332792}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "488ccd76-6a0f-5f9e-a4e7-e1c42b254497" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{\\sqrt{2}/2}^{2}{\\int }_{1/y}^{2y}f(x,y)\\,dx\\,dy" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186332792} :answer-field/id #uuid "80767715-90ed-5a4d-8151-2f2fcea5a376" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186332787}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "37207123-75ea-50cb-aebf-4dad083ae03c" :question/math-academy-id "q-81291" :question/problem "Consider the finite region $D$ in the $xy$-plane enclosed between the curves $y=\\frac{1}{x}$ where $x>0,$ and $y=\\frac{x}{2},$ below the line $y=2.$ What is ${∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\,dA\\,$ expressed as a repeated integral?" :question/worked-solution "First, let's make a sketch of our region.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14055472/assets/2209e57f8cbe84ccf2f930c5cd3004ff5009dfb72f3e4b3262517cf9b8f2b119.png)\n\nWe need to represent $D$ as a type II region. Writing $y=\\frac{1}{x}$ and $y=\\frac{x}{2}$ in terms of $y,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y=\\frac{1}{x} & \\,⟹\\,x=\\frac{1}{y} \\\\ y=\\frac{x}{2} & \\,⟹\\,x=2y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's determine the limits of integration.\n\n- To determine the $y$-limits, we need to find the $y$-coordinates of the points at which the curves intersect. So, we set the following equation and solve it for $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{y} & =2y \\\\ 2y-\\frac{1}{y} & =0 \\\\ \\frac{2{y}^{2}-1}{y} & =0 \\\\ 2{y}^{2}-1 & =0 \\\\ {y}^{2} & =\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ y & =\\pm \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, since our region lies in the first quadrant below the line $y=2$ the $y$-limits are \n$\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\le y\\le 2.$\n\n- To determine the left and right functions, we draw a horizontal arrow from left to right through the region, as usual.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14055472/assets/1b03741537734087836c22dbb40e2e188825bade657cff129374efe1596bcb3c.png)\n\nAs a result, we get\n\n$$\nD={(x,y)\\,:\\,\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\le y\\le 2,\\,\\frac{1}{y}\\le x\\le 2y}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can express the integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\,dA & ={\\int }_{\\sqrt{2}/2}^{2}{\\int }_{1/y}^{2y}f(x,y)\\,dx\\,dy\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186332793 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "fdce40e5-8ffe-52cb-8a8e-ac8eaea982f0" :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating a Repeated Integral Defined Over a Type II Region"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3c11b4dc-13bd-511e-8f5f-7b447c4ebe7a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{sin}⁡(1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186332795} {:answer/id #uuid "34b04a54-facd-5181-be17-e6881fede358" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{sin}⁡(1)+1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186332796} {:answer/id #uuid "a1eaf375-494b-5fad-8cee-15762116cdac" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186332797} {:answer/id #uuid "182210cf-2659-565a-9f73-2a12d001e499" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{sin}⁡(1)-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186332798} {:answer/id #uuid "f1c78e7a-8b16-538d-96a2-da00844af13f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186332799}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "182210cf-2659-565a-9f73-2a12d001e499" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{sin}⁡(1)-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186332798} :answer-field/id #uuid "6ad702c6-d355-5943-9183-a343509e57b9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186332794}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "6c391d3b-2ef4-5185-8c90-e28518247763" :question/math-academy-id "q-81309" :question/problem "${\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{y-1}^{0}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\,dx\\,dy=$" :question/worked-solution "The integral is defined over a type II region.\n\nFirst, we evaluate the inner integral by integrating with respect to $x$, treating $y$ as a constant:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{y-1}^{0}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\,dx\\,dy & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}[{\\int }_{y-1}^{0}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\,dx]\\,dy \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}[-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x]_{y-1}^{0}\\,dy \\\\  & =-{\\int }_{0}^{1}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡0-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(y-1))\\,dy \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(y-1)-1)\\,dy\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we integrate with respect to $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{1}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(y-1)-1)\\,dy & =[\\operatorname{sin}⁡(y-1)-y]_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(0)-1)-(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(-1)-0) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡(1)-1\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186332800 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "fdce40e5-8ffe-52cb-8a8e-ac8eaea982f0" :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating a Repeated Integral Defined Over a Type II Region"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f46cc74f-82a9-511e-af30-ead528b922c7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2-\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186332802} {:answer/id #uuid "224b7702-2e49-5a49-8478-f6c3c5488a3f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186332803} {:answer/id #uuid "ed5525e1-2b59-56b0-98b3-e5d1780a4c11" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186332804} {:answer/id #uuid "d1432c81-1ca0-5ddb-b6fb-151fb9449554" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186332805} {:answer/id #uuid "79af94b3-98d5-5ac9-ba33-b1d8b1ec0c1f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186332806}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "224b7702-2e49-5a49-8478-f6c3c5488a3f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186332803} :answer-field/id #uuid "d5dafd16-e2ce-5543-8186-3d909173e19f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186332801}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "486208d4-0efc-55f9-a8c8-e0ee98659b77" :question/math-academy-id "q-81315" :question/problem "Calculate ${\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{3\\pi /2}}{\\int }_{0}^{y}\\operatorname{cos}⁡{y}^{2}\\,dx\\,dy.$" :question/worked-solution "The integral is defined over a type II region.\n\nFirst, we evaluate the inner integral by integrating with respect to $x$, treating $y$ as a constant:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{3\\pi /2}}{\\int }_{0}^{y}\\operatorname{cos}⁡{y}^{2}\\,dx\\,dy & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{3\\pi /2}}\\operatorname{cos}⁡{y}^{2}[{\\int }_{0}^{y}dx]dy \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{3\\pi /2}}\\operatorname{cos}⁡{y}^{2}[x]_{0}^{y}\\,dy \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{3\\pi /2}}\\operatorname{cos}⁡{y}^{2}(y-0)\\,dy \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{3\\pi /2}}y\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡{y}^{2}\\,dy\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we integrate with respect to $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{3\\pi /2}}y\\operatorname{cos}⁡{y}^{2}\\,dy & ={[\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡{y}^{2}}{2}]}_{0}^{\\sqrt{3\\pi /2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{3\\pi }{2})-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(0)) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(-1-0) \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186332807 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "fdce40e5-8ffe-52cb-8a8e-ac8eaea982f0" :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating a Repeated Integral Defined Over a Type II Region"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f483089b-1921-5111-9b82-95093ed6819e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{17}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186332809} {:answer/id #uuid "7dd5e7bc-235d-5975-a02f-c1d5b119bb5a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{2}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186332810} {:answer/id #uuid "62fcadbd-bd99-5c73-a2b5-702e5c087370" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{7}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186332811} {:answer/id #uuid "ef4ba8e8-1655-50a5-b4ec-735d385a1916" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186332812} {:answer/id #uuid "e5700389-74d9-5655-b605-a75ce4a0605e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{27}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186332813}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e5700389-74d9-5655-b605-a75ce4a0605e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{27}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186332813} :answer-field/id #uuid "9757dc81-7980-5f3f-a73c-c2a54ef6fbbf" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186332808}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "67b5ec5f-9e3b-51f6-93fe-06568326e407" :question/math-academy-id "q-81320" :question/problem "Calculate ${\\int }_{1}^{4}{\\int }_{1}^{{y}^{3}}\\frac{1}{{y}^{2}}\\,dx\\,dy.$" :question/worked-solution "The integral is defined over a type II region.\n\nFirst, we evaluate the inner integral by integrating with respect to $x$, treating $y$ as a constant:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{4}{\\int }_{1}^{{y}^{3}}\\frac{1}{{y}^{2}}\\,dx\\,dy & ={\\int }_{1}^{4}\\frac{1}{{y}^{2}}[{\\int }_{1}^{{y}^{3}}dx]\\,dy \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{4}\\frac{1}{{y}^{2}}[x]_{1}^{{y}^{3}}\\,dy \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{4}\\frac{1}{{y}^{2}}({y}^{3}-1)\\,dy \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{4}(y-\\frac{1}{{y}^{2}})\\,dy\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we integrate with respect to $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{4}(y-\\frac{1}{{y}^{2}})\\,dy & ={[\\frac{{y}^{2}}{2}+\\frac{1}{y}]}_{1}^{4} \\\\  & =(8+\\frac{1}{4})-(\\frac{1}{2}+1) \\\\  & =\\frac{27}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186332814 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "90f7718f-e014-5232-acb1-6773ab79f61d" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating a Double Integral Defined Over a Type II Region"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8f2366f8-c2e9-5fb9-8bdd-90084f93725f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186332816} {:answer/id #uuid "1bcc51c7-00d7-5b95-90a0-8fceb7dcfad7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186332817} {:answer/id #uuid "37864aa5-0f9a-5c72-93f5-f3617cc715fd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186332818} {:answer/id #uuid "7bda23f6-6f68-5d9a-a1e0-47e23b7213d6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "18" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186332819} {:answer/id #uuid "41d4a459-68c0-53ce-80c8-e512dcb0f122" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186332820}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "37864aa5-0f9a-5c72-93f5-f3617cc715fd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186332818} :answer-field/id #uuid "8549113f-232a-5575-8e07-f9e818e96622" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186332815}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "9e6f245d-e90c-5a95-8ef4-6c1feb736768" :question/math-academy-id "q-81395" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14055472/assets/ff3b9f52ed01d991d1fff18e78c494488b53703669b6bb326b5436a19c952a18.png)\n\nEvaluate the double integral ${∬}_{D}x\\,dA,\\,$\nwhere $D$ is the region enclosed between the curves $3{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=9$ and ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=9,$ as shown in the picture." :question/worked-solution "Notice that $D$ is a type II region. Since $x\\ge 0,$ we obtain the following equations in terms of $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=9 & \\,⟹\\,x=\\sqrt{9-{y}^{2}} \\\\ 3{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=9 & \\,⟹\\,x=\\sqrt{3-\\frac{{y}^{2}}{3}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's determine the limits of integration.\n\n- To determine the $y$-limits, we need to find the $y$-coordinates of the points at which the curves intersect. So, we set the following equation and solve it for $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{9-{y}^{2}} & =\\sqrt{3-\\frac{{y}^{2}}{3}} \\\\ 9-{y}^{2} & =3-\\frac{{y}^{2}}{3} \\\\ \\frac{2}{3}{y}^{2} & =6 \\\\ {y}^{2} & =9\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, since our region lies above the line the $x$-axis, the $y$-limits are \n$0\\le y\\le 3.$\n\n- To determine the left and right functions, we draw a horizontal arrow from left to right through the region, as usual.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14055472/assets/91f377562a1e94218b7be886cd63638e8881b0c4fac231a810bb6bff3a1fe643.png)\n\nAs a result, we get\n\n$$\nD={(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le y\\le 3,\\,\\sqrt{3-\\frac{{y}^{2}}{3}}\\le x\\le \\sqrt{9-{y}^{2}}}.\n$$\n\nSo, we can express the integral as follows:\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}x\\,dA={\\int }_{0}^{3}{\\int }_{\\sqrt{3-{y}^{2}/3}}^{\\sqrt{9-{y}^{2}}}x\\,dx\\,dy\n$$\n\nFirst, we evaluate the inner integral by integrating with respect to $x$, treating $y$ as a constant:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{3}{\\int }_{\\sqrt{3-{y}^{2}/3}}^{\\sqrt{9-{y}^{2}}}x\\,dx\\,dy & ={\\int }_{0}^{3}[{\\int }_{\\sqrt{3-{y}^{2}/3}}^{\\sqrt{9-{y}^{2}}}x\\,dx]dy \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{3}{[\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}]}_{\\sqrt{3-{y}^{2}/3}}^{\\sqrt{9-{y}^{2}}}\\,dy \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{3}\\frac{1}{2}(9-{y}^{2}-(3-\\frac{{y}^{2}}{3}))\\,dy \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{3}(6-\\frac{2}{3}{y}^{2})\\,dy\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we integrate with respect to $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{3}(6-\\frac{2}{3}{y}^{2})\\,dy & =\\frac{1}{2}{[6y-\\frac{2}{9}{y}^{3}]}_{0}^{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(18-6-0) \\\\  & =6\\end{aligned}\n$$"}]}
